abstract: >-
  A satellite study of vegetated karewa tableland lost to brick kilns in the
  Kashmir Valley. An earlier version reported that the bare-earth share of
  karewa terraces rose from 1.84% in 1994 to 8.43% in 2025; that result came
  from comparing Landsat composites with a differently built Sentinel-2
  composite and is withdrawn. This version maps 180 scarp-bounded karewa
  terraces (173.3 square kilometres) from the Copernicus elevation model using
  slope, height above drainage and scarp share, checks the map against a
  published geological map, and follows the yearly peak NDVI of every 30 m
  pixel using Landsat only. Between 2013-2015 and 2023-2025 a strict test
  finds 107 ha of terrace land converted from vegetated to bare and a looser
  test 329 ha. Two stratified random samples, 240 hand-labelled points in
  all, show that about 262 ha of the flagged land was vegetated in imagery of
  2013-2015 and is not today, and that about 210 ha (95% interval roughly
  189-231 ha) lies inside brick-kiln fields, concentrated in two belts in
  Budgam district, Rangeen Kultreh and Bandagam-Batapora. Depth of excavation could not be measured,
  and no link to the saffron tablelands of Pampore was found.
keywords:
  - karewa
  - Kashmir Valley
  - brick kilns
  - land conversion
  - Landsat time series
  - sensor consistency
  - accuracy assessment
  - Google Earth Engine
